# Data, ledger and sync

Read `docs/DATA-MODEL.md` and `docs/OFFLINE-SYNC.md` before touching anything
here. Schema changes belong to Claude, not to an IDE session — if a fix needs a
migration, stop and hand it over.

## The ledger

```
boa_bar_movement is append-only.
No UPDATE. No DELETE. No TRUNCATE.
Corrections create compensating movements.
```

Stock on hand is derived by **summing the ledger**. There is no authoritative
quantity column. `private.boa_bar_balance` is a projection, and it is only safe
under the five conditions in `docs/DATA-MODEL.md` — written solely by the posting
function, no client grants, a reconciliation view with a test, and a
ledger-summing fallback for every read path.

## The identity that must hold

```
Opening + Received − Issued out + Received in − Sold − Comped − Wasted − Returned = Closing
```

Per SKU, per location, per window — and it must be computable in SQL, decomposed
by movement kind.

## Movement rules

| Kind | Line sum |
| --- | --- |
| `issue`, `transfer`, `return`, `comp` | Two legs netting to **zero** |
| `receipt` | Net **positive** |
| `sale`, `waste` | Net **negative** |
| `adjustment` | Signed, reason required, manager only |

`comp` is a custody move to a hospitality location, **not** a depletion.
Hospitality depletion must never enter sales variance.

`sale` is writable only by the POS import path. Never hand-keyed.

## Units

Capture **both** `container_delta` and `ml_delta` at write time. Derive neither
from the other at read time — the point is that the ledger proves they agree.
Deriving one makes the cross-check vacuous and makes partial containers unmovable.

## Variance

```
theoretical = opening + in − out − sold − comped − wasted
variance_ml = counted − theoretical
variance_pct = variance_ml / (sold + comped + wasted)
```

Percentage of **throughput**, never of stock held. Banding is **signed** — never
`Math.abs()` before banding. Positive variance means a missed receipt, a
wrong-SKU ring-up, or an unaccepted docket. It is not good news and must not be
green.

## Blind counting

Count inputs start at zero. While a count session is open for location L and user
U, U must not be able to read L's expected position through any path — not the
snapshot RPC, not by summing the raw ledger. This is **count-scoped, not
role-scoped**: crew may see stock in general, but not for the location they are
counting.

The theoretical position is frozen server-side at submission
(`count_session.sealed_position`) so variance is reproducible. A submitted count
is sealed — the counter cannot edit it after the reveal.

## Offline

- Every write goes to the durable outbox, online or offline. No direct-to-network path.
- Idempotency keys are minted **once per user action** and persisted, then reused
  on retry. A key regenerated per call protects against nothing.
- Replay is **strictly ordered** — a failed entry blocks the ones behind it, or an
  acceptance can post before its issue.
- A failed live load reads the reference cache. An empty cache shows an explicit
  empty state — **never** fixture data rendered as real stock.
- No `void` promise that can swallow a throw. Never toast success before the
  write is durable.
- A permanently-invalid entry goes to a dead-letter state a human can inspect —
  it does not retry forever.
- Nothing mutable lives only in React memory. A reload mid-count loses nothing.

## Numbers

Money is integer minor units (paise). Quantities are `numeric` / `integer` /
`bigint`. **No float, anywhere, ever.**

## Steppers and quantity inputs

Every quantity input needs a **maximum**: you cannot issue more than the
warehouse holds, and you cannot accept more than was issued. An over-receipt must
not be silently classified as a shortfall.

## Testing

Existence assertions prove nothing. Tests must exercise behaviour: attempt an
UPDATE, a DELETE and a TRUNCATE and assert they fail; connect as `authenticated`
with a real role claim and assert each policy's actual effect; post the same
action twice and assert one row; post an unmapped POS SKU and assert the batch is
rejected.
